    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Beaver, Arkansas, takes its name from Squire Wilson Ashbury Beaver, born June 7, 1831, who helped found the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://carrollcountyar.com/beaver.php"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s iconic Beaver Bridge is a two‑panel suspension bridge over the White River, listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaver%2C_Arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A late‑1800s railroad trestle across the White River in Beaver was featured in the 1982 Civil War film 'Blue & Gray'.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.beavertownarkansas.com/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Beaver’s historic little bridge also appeared in the 2005 film 'Elizabethtown,' showcasing the town’s picturesque scenery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.beavertownarkansas.com/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Beaver Family built Beaver’s Ferry and Beaver’s Station, making the area a key stop‑over for stagecoaches in the late 1800s.",
